          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Urine infections are bacterial infections of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, which is called a lower tract infection or cystitis. *Escherichia coli* causes most of them. (Source: Urinary tract infection, general clinical reference.) Infection that reaches the kidney is a different and more serious illness.\n\nHaving infections more than once is a pattern worth raising with a clinician. Doctors often treat a first infection differently from repeated ones, and in some cases look for an underlying cause. The guidance I could retrieve also separates uncomplicated infection, in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function, from complicated infection. Complicated cases include pregnancy, men, catheters, kidney stones, structural differences, diabetes with poor control, and weakened immunity. The same bug can be managed differently depending on which applies. (Sources: Urinary tract infection; Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guidance.)\n\nThe material I could find does not cover the common causes or risk factors for recurrent infections, or specific prevention measures. The lifestyle search returned nothing on urinary infections. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge here, so a clinician or a reliable patient information resource is the best next step for those questions.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you have any of these:** fever or chills, confusion, feeling very unwell or drowsy, fast breathing, or pain in the back or side. These can signal a serious response to infection, which can affect people with urinary infections. (Source: Sepsis guidance.)\n\n**Before your appointment, it may help to note:**\n- How often infections happen and roughly when\n- Your symptoms each time and how they settled\n- Any tests or treatments you have had before\n- Any other health conditions and medicines you take\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 8249.600641000143,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
          "red_flag_negation_suppressed": false,
