          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: not every time.** Antibiotics treat bacterial infections. Many common infections are viral, and antibiotics do not work against viruses.\n\n**Viral infections.** Most colds, most sore throats, acute bronchitis and early uncomplicated sinusitis are viral in most cases. Antibiotics do not shorten them or prevent complications at a rate that justifies routine use. They also carry real harms, including side effects, allergic reactions, bowel infections such as *C. difficile*, and antibiotic resistance. (Sources: Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Upper respiratory infection (common cold).)\n\n**Signs that do not mean a bacterial infection.** Guidance lists these as not indicating a bacterial infection on their own:\n- Yellow or green nasal discharge, which is part of the normal viral course\n- A cold lasting seven to ten days\n- A cough lasting two to three weeks after a cold\n- Low-grade fever in the first few days\n\n(Source: Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance.)\n\n**When a clinician might still prescribe one.** Guidance describes antibiotics as indicated for some cases, such as acute bacterial sinusitis. That diagnosis rests on specific patterns, not symptoms alone. One pattern is symptoms lasting ten days or more without improvement. Another is severe symptoms with high fever and thick discharge. For urinary infections, the choice depends on whether the infection is uncomplicated or complicated. (Sources: Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guidance.)\n\nThe right answer depends on the specific infection, your symptoms, and how long they have lasted. The retrieved passages do not cover every infection, so I cannot say whether an antibiotic would be right for you. If you tell me the symptoms or the condition you are asking about, I can explain what the guidance says for that situation.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have fever with confusion, feel very unwell or drowsy, breathe fast, or have back or side pain. These can signal a serious response to infection. (Source: Sepsis guidance.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment, condition-urinary-tract-infection, red-flag-sepsis]",
